1. Third-party material carried in this repository
--------------------------------------------------

payload/rime-japanese/japanese_tw_eng.schema.yaml
payload/rime-japanese/sno_japanese.schema.yaml

    Composed for this plugin, and derived works of

        https://github.com/snomiao/rime-snomiao
        Rime/sno_jpn_wubi86_eng.schema.yaml
        at revision a2f3e7c7505abe567ec282ce61f754885c413e88

    Neither file exists upstream under these names, but most of what makes them
    work is upstream's: the speller algebra, the `japanese_lookup`,
    `japanese_pinyin_lookup`, `translate_en2jp_lookup` and reverse-lookup
    blocks, the `comment_format` chains, the `editor` binding, the `menu`
    block. sno_japanese's romaji half traces further back, to

        https://github.com/gkovacs/rime-japanese
        japanese.schema.yaml
        at revision 4c1e65135459175136f380e90ba52acb40fdfb2d

    Both files carry that attribution in their own headers, and the `author:`
    lists inside them are upstream's own, with the names and addresses upstream
    wrote.

    Terms: see section 3 - neither repository ships a licence file.

payload/rime-japanese/opencc/jpzh.json

    An OpenCC configuration derived from snomiao's Rime/opencc/zhjp.json at the
    revision above: the same segmentation and conversion chain over the same two
    dictionaries, with the `name` field changed. It exists because snomiao's own
    schema names `jpzh.json` and the repository does not ship one. JSON has no
    comment syntax, so this entry is its only attribution.

    Terms: see section 3.

payload/rime-japanese/opencc/hiragana_katakana.json
payload/rime-japanese/opencc/hiragana_katakana.txt

    An OpenCC configuration and its dictionary, mapping each hiragana to its
    katakana. **Provenance not fully established.** No file of either name
    exists in either pinned upstream, and a search turned up no repository that
    publishes them under these names; the nearest thing found - momijineko's
    Rime-KappaJP - uses different filenames (`hira2kata.json`, `hirakata.ocd`).
    The .txt is the mechanical Unicode hiragana-to-katakana correspondence, 84
    lines of it, which is a fact rather than an authored table; the .json is
    OpenCC's ordinary config skeleton with those two filenames in it. Both are
    therefore treated as written here. If anyone recognises them as theirs,
    please open an issue and they will be credited or removed.

payload/fcitx5/config
payload/fcitx5/conf/*.conf

    Fcitx5 settings in Fcitx5's own configuration format, produced by Fcitx5's
    own configuration writer on a configured machine and then trimmed to what
    this plugin has an opinion about. The values are this plugin's choices; the
    `# ...` comment above each key is the option label Fcitx5 itself emits.
    Fcitx5 is LGPL-2.1-or-later.


2. Not third-party material, despite appearances
-------------------------------------------------

tests/fixtures/cedict-sample.u8

    A test fixture written for this repository, in CC-CEDICT's line format. It
    is **not** CC-CEDICT and contains no CC-CEDICT entries. An earlier version
    of this file was a verbatim excerpt of the published dictionary, header and
    all; it was replaced, because a test needs the format and not upstream's
    entries. The format itself - comment lines, a `#!` metadata block, then
    `Traditional Simplified [pin1 yin1] /sense/sense/` - is not anybody's
    property. The file's own header says all of this too.

payload/rime/lua/annotate.lua

    Written for this plugin. It parses CC-CEDICT's published line format; it
    embeds none of its data. The counts in its comments are measurements taken
    over the published file, not extracts from it.


3. What is known about the two Rime upstreams' terms
-----------------------------------------------------

https://github.com/gkovacs/rime-japanese

    **No licence.** There is no LICENSE or COPYING file at the pinned revision,
    the README says nothing about terms, and GitHub's own repository metadata
    reports no licence. No permission to redistribute has been granted by the
    author, and the author has not been contacted. This repository therefore
    redistributes none of its files: everything from it is fetched from GitHub
    onto the user's own machine at deploy time (section 4). What is carried here
    is sno_japanese.schema.yaml, whose romaji half derives from that repository
    and which credits it.

https://github.com/snomiao/rime-snomiao

    **No licence file; an ISC declaration in package.json.** There is no LICENSE
    or COPYING file at the pinned revision and GitHub reports no licence, but
    the repository's package.json carries `"license": "ISC"` and a `"files"`
    list that publishes the `Rime` directory to npm. That is a deliberate
    statement of terms by the author over the material this plugin uses, and it
    is what the two derived schema files and jpzh.json rely on. It is worth
    being plain that a package.json field is a weaker artefact than a licence
    text: it is what the author declared for the published package, no more.
    The author has not been contacted.

Because neither upstream ships a licence file, the plugin's rule is to
redistribute nothing from either of them. Every file it needs from them is
fetched at deploy time, byte for byte, from the publisher's own repository at a
pinned commit - see lib/assets.py, `SOURCE_FILES`. Four files used to be shipped
here as edited copies and are not any more:

    japanese.dict.yaml         (from gkovacs/rime-japanese)
    japanese_pinyin.schema.yaml   (from snomiao/rime-snomiao)
    translate_en2jp.schema.yaml   (from snomiao/rime-snomiao)
    opencc/zhjp.json              (from snomiao/rime-snomiao)


